<SEOArticle title="10-Minute Full-Body Mobility Routine" description="Follow a simple 10-minute full-body mobility routine for ankles, hips, upper back and shoulders, with beginner modifications and safety guidance." canonical="/recovery/10-minute-mobility-routine/" eyebrow="Daily Mobility" heading="A simple 10-minute full-body mobility routine." lead="Move through comfortable ranges, breathe normally and finish feeling more prepared—not exhausted. No special equipment is required beyond a stable chair or wall for support." {faqs} {toc} secondaryLabel="Workout Recovery Guide" secondaryHref="/recovery/mobility-recovery-after-workouts/">
 <p>Use about one minute for each step. Move more slowly when a drill includes two sides. This routine is general education and is not designed to diagnose or treat pain.</p>
 <h2 id="routine">The 10-minute mobility routine</h2>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Breathing and reach.</strong> Stand or sit comfortably. Take several quiet breaths while reaching the arms forward, overhead or only as high as comfortable.</li>
  <li><strong>Ankle rocks.</strong> With support, move one knee gently forward over the foot while the heel remains down. Alternate sides.</li>
  <li><strong>Supported squat.</strong> Hold a stable surface and sit the hips down within a comfortable range. Stand with control.</li>
  <li><strong>Hip hinge.</strong> Soften the knees, send the hips backward and keep the movement controlled through the trunk.</li>
  <li><strong>Split-stance hip movement.</strong> Step one foot back and gently shift forward and backward without forcing the front of the hip.</li>
  <li><strong>Upper-back rotation.</strong> Sit or stand tall and rotate the ribcage gently from side to side.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoulder circles.</strong> Move the shoulders slowly forward and backward, then reach the arms through a comfortable path.</li>
  <li><strong>Wall or desk push-away.</strong> Place the hands on a stable surface, move the hips backward and allow the chest to lower only as comfortable.</li>
  <li><strong>Step and balance.</strong> March slowly or practice supported single-leg balance for several seconds per side.</li>
  <li><strong>Walk and reassess.</strong> Walk for the final minute and notice whether movement feels different.</li>
 </ol>
 <h2 id="modify">How to modify the routine</h2>
 <div class="grid two">
  <div class="card"><h3>Make it easier</h3><p>Use support, reduce range, slow down, remain seated or perform fewer repetitions.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><h3>Make it more specific</h3><p>Spend extra time on an area relevant to the upcoming activity instead of making every drill harder.</p></div>
 </div>
 <div class="callout"><strong>Mobility is not a flexibility contest.</strong> Control and relevance matter more than reaching the largest possible range.</div>
 <h2 id="timing">When to use this routine</h2>
 <ul><li>In the morning as a general movement check-in</li><li>During a workday after prolonged sitting</li><li>Before an easy activity</li><li>On a recovery day</li><li>After travel</li></ul>
 <p>Before demanding strength, speed or sport training, follow it with a gradual activity-specific warmup.</p>
 <h2 id="progress">How to progress mobility</h2>
 <p>Repeat the routine consistently before adding complexity. Progress can mean smoother control, easier breathing, greater comfort or a slightly larger usable range—not simply a more extreme position.</p>
 <h2>When to skip a movement</h2>
 <p>Stop or modify for sharp, radiating or worsening symptoms, dizziness, new weakness or loss of balance. Persistent limitations or pain deserve an appropriate professional assessment.</p>
 <h2>Continue with guided body care</h2>
 <p>Learn how mobility fits with warmups, cooldowns and recovery in the <a href="/body-care/">Body Care guide</a>.</p>
